<p class="cl-lead">There is no diploma on the wall that says "licensed to line sewers." Cured in place pipe training and certifications are a patchwork: a state license that permits the work, manufacturer training that makes it competent, and industry programs that show a company takes the craft seriously. This page is written for the property owner trying to judge a contractor, not for contractors trying to enter the trade. Here is what to look for, roughly in order of importance.</p>

<h2>1. A current state contractor license in the right classification</h2>
<p>Lining a sewer is sewer work, and every state regulates who may do it. In most states that is a plumbing contractor license or a sewer, sanitation or underground utility classification; the names differ, the idea does not. Look the license up on your state licensing board's website and confirm three things: it is <strong>active</strong>, it is in a <strong>classification that covers sewer or plumbing work</strong>, and it belongs to the <strong>company that will perform the job</strong>. A general contractor subcontracting the lining to an unnamed crew is a question to ask, not necessarily a dealbreaker. Every installer listed on this site has had its license checked as part of the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/near-me">verification</a> process.</p>

<h2>2. Manufacturer training on the system they install</h2>
<p>This is the credential that most directly predicts a good liner. Each liner and resin system has its own wet-out procedure, installation pressures, cure schedule and cool-down rules. Manufacturers train and certify the installers who buy their systems, and many will honor the material warranty only when a trained installer did the work. Ask two questions: <em>Which liner and resin system do you install?</em> and <em>Can you show me your training or certification from that manufacturer?</em> A lining contractor answers both in a sentence. Hesitation, or "we use whatever the supplier has," is a signal. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/equipment">cured in place pipe equipment</a> page describes the gear each system requires, which is another way to check the answer.</p>

<h2>3. Industry programs</h2>
<h3>NASSCO ITCP for CIPP</h3>
<p>The National Association of Sewer Service Companies runs an Inspector Training and Certification Program with a CIPP track. It is a two-day course, about 11.5 hours of instruction plus a two-hour test with an 85 percent pass mark, covering how existing defects affect a liner, CIPP technology, field installation, how to read and write performance specifications, and where other renewal methods fit. It was designed for the inspectors who sign off on public lining projects. When a contractor's project managers or foremen hold it, they have learned the job from the inspector's side of the table, which is exactly what you want from the person checking your cure log.</p>
<h3>NASSCO PACP</h3>
<p>The Pipeline Assessment Certification Program is a standardized way of coding what a camera sees. A PACP-certified operator describes your pipe in the same terms a city engineer would use, which makes the inspection report precise and comparable. If the report you receive uses PACP codes, the operator was trained to this standard.</p>
<h3>NASTT courses</h3>
<p>The North American Society for Trenchless Technology publishes good-practice guidelines and runs courses on CIPP and other trenchless methods. Membership and course attendance show a company that keeps up with the field.</p>

<h2>4. Experience you can see</h2>
<p>Credentials say a company can do the work. Completed projects show that it does. Ask how many pipes like yours they have lined in the last year, and ask to see before-and-after recordings from a similar job. Installers on this site post <a href="/all-contractors">completed projects with the details</a>: material, diameter, footage, what was wrong, how it was fixed and, where the owner had another bid, what the saving was. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/cost">cured in place pipe cost</a> page gathers those numbers.</p>

<h2>5. The paperwork they hand you</h2>
<p>At the end of a well-run job you should be holding, without having to chase anyone:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The <strong>pre-lining camera recording</strong> and the <strong>post-lining recording</strong>.</li>
  <li>The <strong>cure log</strong>: time and temperature, or the UV controller's output.</li>
  <li>The <strong>liner and resin system</strong> installed, by name, and its design thickness.</li>
  <li>The <strong>installer's written workmanship warranty</strong> and any <strong>manufacturer material warranty</strong>.</li>
  <li>An <strong>invoice</strong> that lists footage lined and connections reinstated.</li>
</ul>
<p>On public work the list is longer, including lab results from a sample of the liner; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/liner-testing">cured in place pipe liner testing</a> page explains those and what a homeowner can reasonably ask for instead.</p>

<h2>What the CIPP Verified listing is, and is not</h2>
<p>Installers on CuredInPlacePipe.net carry a CIPP Verified badge. It is a <strong>listing</strong>, not a training program or a trade certification. To be listed, a company must already perform cured in place pipe work, have at least five years in business and a strong public review record, hold a current license, document its lining history and its training on the system it installs, and complete a phone interview. The badge tells a property owner that someone has already checked the items on this page. It does not teach anyone to line pipe, and companies looking to get into the trade should go to the liner manufacturers and to NASSCO and NASTT, not here. Contractors who already qualify can <a href="/apply">apply to be listed</a>.</p>

